      "id": 1,
      "text": "Riddleville earned its curious name from a simple coin toss by James Page, who flipped a penny to decide the town’s name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitwacoga.org/riddleville.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was officially incorporated by the Georgia General Assembly in 1859, marking its formal start as a municipality.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riddleville%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Homes dating back to 1850 line the streets, surrounded by mature trees that give Riddleville a peaceful, country‑like feel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitwacoga.org/riddleville.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1917 J.Y. Ryan & Sons commercial building still stands at the town’s main intersection, a relic of Riddleville’s early commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitwacoga.org/riddleville.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Riddleville once hosted the Riddleville Academ, one of Washington County’s four academies, which later became the Mount Vernon Institute historic home.",
